meta {
  name: Reject - private address
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/links
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "destination_url": "http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/"
  }
}

tests {
  test("refused as private_address", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(422);
    expect(res.body.error.code).to.equal("private_address");
  });
}

docs {
  research.md D11, and the reason the validator exists: the cloud metadata
  endpoint. The rejection comes from the *address*, never from the name — the
  host is resolved and every address it returns is checked, so a hostname the
  attacker controls pointing at 127.0.0.1 is refused just the same.

  Twenty-two ranges are blocked. Others worth pasting in: `http://127.0.0.1/`,
  `http://10.0.0.7/`, `http://[::1]/`, `http://[::ffff:127.0.0.1]/`,
  `http://192.168.1.1/`.

  A literal IP is judged without any DNS lookup at all.
}
